  "slug": "delivery-gene-a136",
  "summary": "Wild-type AAV9 and barcoded AAV-KP capsid screens now permit targeted renal transduction in mice, including macula densa cells.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is AAV-mediated gene transfer into defined kidney cell populations in mice. Wild-type AAV9 is reported to transduce macula densa cells in neonatal and adult mice, supporting cell-specific gain- and loss-of-function models of renin control. Separately, retrograde delivery through the renal pelvis, applied to a barcoded AAV library, identified AAV-KP capsids that transduce mouse kidney broadly, and the same work assesses transgene reabsorption in the kidney. The new elements are route-level and capsid-level selection for renal targeting. A third item, single-nucleus RNA-seq of mouse and human connecting tubules, is a separate study of calcium, sodium, and water transport programs that provides cell-type resolution relevant to target selection rather than delivery itself.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
